.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/eslint-scope Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
[![npm version](https://img.shields.io/npm/v/eslint-scope.svg)](https://www.npmjs.com/package/eslint-scope) \fR\fP \fR\fP
.SH "ESLint Scope"
.PP
ESLint Scope is the \fRECMAScript\fP scope analyzer used in ESLint\&. It is a fork of \fRescope\fP\&.
.SS "Install"
.PP
.nf
npm i eslint\-scope \-\-save
.fi
.PP
.SS "📖 Usage"
To use in an ESM file:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import * as eslintScope from 'eslint\-scope';
.fi
.PP

.PP
To use in a CommonJS file:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const eslintScope = require('eslint\-scope');
.fi
.PP

.PP
In order to analyze scope, you'll need to have an \fRESTree\fP compliant AST structure to run it on\&. The primary method is \fReslintScope\&.analyze()\fP, which takes two arguments:

.PP
.IP "1." 4
\fRast\fP - the ESTree-compliant AST structure to analyze\&.
.IP "2." 4
\fRoptions\fP (optional) - Options to adjust how the scope is analyzed, including:
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRignoreEval\fP (default: \fRfalse\fP) - Set to \fRtrue\fP to ignore all \fReval()\fP calls (which would normally create scopes)\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRnodejsScope\fP (default: \fRfalse\fP) - Set to \fRtrue\fP to create a top-level function scope needed for CommonJS evaluation\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRimpliedStrict\fP (default: \fRfalse\fP) - Set to \fRtrue\fP to evaluate the code in strict mode even outside of modules and without \fR"use strict"\fP\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRecmaVersion\fP (default: \fR5\fP) - The version of ECMAScript to use to evaluate the code\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRsourceType\fP (default: \fR"script"\fP) - The type of JavaScript file to evaluate\&. Change to \fR"module"\fP for ECMAScript module code\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRchildVisitorKeys\fP (default: \fRnull\fP) - An object with visitor key information (like \fR\fReslint-visitor-keys\fP\fP)\&. Without this, \fReslint-scope\fP finds child nodes to visit algorithmically\&. Providing this option is a performance enhancement\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRfallback\fP (default: \fR"iteration"\fP) - The strategy to use when \fRchildVisitorKeys\fP is not specified\&. May be a function\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRjsx\fP (default: \fRfalse\fP) - Enables the tracking of JSX components as variable references\&.
.PP

.PP

.PP
Example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import * as eslintScope from 'eslint\-scope';
import * as espree from 'espree';
import estraverse from 'estraverse';

const options = {
    ecmaVersion: 2022,
    sourceType: "module"
};

const ast = espree\&.parse(code, { range: true, \&.\&.\&.options });
const scopeManager = eslintScope\&.analyze(ast, options);

const currentScope = scopeManager\&.acquire(ast);   // global scope

estraverse\&.traverse(ast, {
    enter (node, parent) {
        // do stuff

        if (/Function/\&.test(node\&.type)) {
            currentScope = scopeManager\&.acquire(node);  // get current function scope
        }
    },
    leave(node, parent) {
        if (/Function/\&.test(node\&.type)) {
            currentScope = currentScope\&.upper;  // set to parent scope
        }

        // do stuff
    }
});
.fi
.PP
.SS "API"
The following section describes the API for this package\&. You can also read \fRthe docs\fP\&.
.SS "ScopeManager"
The \fRScopeManager\fP class is at the core of eslint-scope and is returned when you call \fReslintScope\&.analyze()\fP\&. It manages all scopes in a given AST\&.
.SS "Properties"
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRscopes\fP - An array of all scopes\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRglobalScope\fP - Reference to the global scope\&.
.PP
.SS "Methods"
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fB\fRacquire(node, inner)\fP\fP Acquires the appropriate scope for a given node\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRnode\fP - The AST node to acquire the scope from\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRinner\fP - Optional boolean\&. When \fRtrue\fP, returns the innermost scope, otherwise returns the outermost scope\&. Default is \fRfalse\fP\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
Returns: The acquired scope or \fRnull\fP if no scope is found\&.
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fB\fRacquireAll(node)\fP\fP Acquires all scopes for a given node\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRnode\fP - The AST node to acquire scopes from\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
Returns: An array of scopes or \fRundefined\fP if none are found\&.
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fB\fRrelease(node, inner)\fP\fP Returns the upper scope for a given node\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRnode\fP - The AST node to release\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRinner\fP - Optional boolean\&. When \fRtrue\fP, returns the innermost upper scope, otherwise returns the outermost upper scope\&. Default is \fRfalse\fP\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
Returns: The upper scope or \fRnull\fP if no upper scope exists\&.
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fB\fRgetDeclaredVariables(node)\fP\fP Get variables that are declared by the node\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRnode\fP - The AST node to get declarations from\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
Returns: An array of variable objects declared by the node\&. If the node doesn't declare any variables, it returns an empty array\&.
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fB\fRisGlobalReturn()\fP\fP Determines if the global return statement should be allowed\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
Returns: \fRtrue\fP if the global return is enabled\&.
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fB\fRisModule()\fP\fP Checks if the code should be handled as an ECMAScript module\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
Returns: \fRtrue\fP if the sourceType is "module"\&.
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fB\fRisImpliedStrict()\fP\fP Checks if implied strict mode is enabled\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
Returns: \fRtrue\fP if implied strict mode is enabled\&.
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fB\fRisStrictModeSupported()\fP\fP Checks if strict mode is supported based on ECMAScript version\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
Returns: \fRtrue\fP if the ECMAScript version supports strict mode\&.
.PP

.PP
.SS "Scope Objects"
Scopes returned by the ScopeManager methods have the following properties:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRtype\fP - The type of scope (e\&.g\&., "function", "block", "global")\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRvariables\fP - Array of variables declared in this scope\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRset\fP - A Map of variable names to Variable objects for variables declared in this scope\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRreferences\fP - Array of references in this scope\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRthrough\fP - Array of references in this scope and its child scopes that aren't resolved in this scope or its child scopes\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRvariableScope\fP - Reference to the closest variable scope\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRupper\fP - Reference to the parent scope\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRchildScopes\fP - Array of child scopes\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRblock\fP - The AST node that created this scope\&.
.PP
.SS "GlobalScope"
The \fRGlobalScope\fP class is a specialized scope representing the global execution context\&. It extends the base \fRScope\fP class with additional functionality for handling implicitly defined global variables\&.
.SS "Properties"
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fB\fRimplicit\fP\fP - Tracks implicitly defined global variables (those used without declaration)\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRset\fP - A Map of variable names to Variable objects for implicitly defined globals\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRvariables\fP - Array of implicit global Variable objects\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRleft\fP - Array of References that need to be linked to the variable they refer to\&.
.PP

.PP
.SS "Variable Objects"
Each variable object has the following properties:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRname\fP - The variable name\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRidentifiers\fP - Array of identifier nodes declaring this variable\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRreferences\fP - Array of references to this variable\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRdefs\fP - Array of definition objects for this variable\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRscope\fP - The scope object where this variable is defined\&.
.PP
